<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128479" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/04144359/The_Engine_Gulliver.png" alt width="640" height="675" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/04144359/The_Engine_Gulliver.png 640w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/04144359/The_Engine_Gulliver-341x360.png 341w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/04144359/The_Engine_Gulliver-228x240.png 228w" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px"></p>
<p>The printing press was invented in the middle of the fifteenth century, and it’s been downhill for the written word ever since. Even before then, arguments that the volume of writing being done far outstripped the value of that writing surely weren’t unheard of. Whatever the era, the most incisive remarks on the apparent victory of verbal quantity over verbal quality tend to have come from writers themselves. No less a satirist than <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonathan_Swift">Jonathan Swift</a> made it one of his many targets in&nbsp;<a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/files/829/829-h/829-h.htm"><em>Gulliver’s Travels</em></a> (1726), imagining an elaborate machine of wood and wire called “the Engine” (see illustration above) that automatically generates text, though few remember it — or rather, few remembered it until the rise of large language models in the real world.</p>
<p>Gulliver encounters that technological marvel, the Engine, at an institution called the Academy of Projectors in the city of Lagado. Swift’s protagonist records its creator’s explanation as follows: “Every one knew how laborious the usual method is of attaining to arts and sciences; whereas, by his contrivance, the most ignorant person, at a reasonable charge, and with a little bodily labor, might write books in philosophy, poetry, politics, laws, mathematics, and theology, without the least assistance from genius or study.” The Engine’s operation, with its unwieldy, clacking components and a scrambling staff of young crankers, readers, and scribes, may strike us as bizarre, just as it did audiences in the eighteenth century. But in our time, when students can write their papers literally without giving it a thought (to say nothing of what their professors may be getting up to), its concept now sounds all too plausible.</p>
